WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.025
Now the numbers and lines in the water.

2
00:00:03.275 --> 00:00:06.975
Soundings are the small numbers scattered over the water.

3
00:00:07.225 --> 00:00:12.050
Each is a depth below the chart datum, usually mean lower low water.

4
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:19.850
Depth contours are lines that join points of equal depth, so you can see the edge of a shoal or a channel at a glance.

5
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.600
Small letters describe the bottom.

6
00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:29.375
S is sand, M is mud, S h is shells, R k is rock, C o is coral, and G is gravel.

7
00:00:29.625 --> 00:00:34.525
Qualifiers go with them: h r d for hard, s f t for soft.

8
00:00:34.775 --> 00:00:37.700
Why would you care what the bottom is made of?

9
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.225
Anchoring.

10
00:00:40.475 --> 00:00:43.050
Sand and mud hold an anchor well.

11
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.325
Rock and coral don’t.

12
00:00:45.575 --> 00:00:47.350
And one caution.

13
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.300
A charted depth is only as good as the last survey, and bottoms move.

14
00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:55.375
Near Florida’s inlets, they move a lot.
